{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's central causal claim—that two critics failing the same artifact 'most economical[ly]' implies a shared structural assumption rather than a shared reasoning error—is an unsupported false dichotomy. The argument rules out (b) shared reasoning error by bare assertion ('rather than'), yet critics can independently arrive at the same *correct* failure verdict through sound reasoning, making both verdicts true positives rather than evidence of any deficiency. SRC_001 conflates 'both failed' (critic error) with 'both produced the same verdict-class' (which includes both being right), then uses that conflation to conclude the test is degenerate—a non sequitur that does not actually sustain a §11.3 flag against the critics.","premise":"SRC_001 presents a false dichotomy between shared structural assumption and test-design deficiency, ignoring that two critics can independently and correctly identify the same genuine flaw. Because this third possibility is never considered, the claim that repeated failure is 'actually evidence of a test-design deficiency' does not follow from the premise of two independent failure verdicts.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"0c61961a8085","quote":"the most economical causal explanation is a shared structural assumption ... rather than a shared reasoning error"},{"block":"0c61961a8085","quote":"The flag persists because the audit procedure treats repeated failure as evidence of critic deficiency when it is actually evidence of a test-design deficiency"}],"target":"2b596ab6b62847caffaa38bbc9e4d56efd9b6f298440f540389e33f691d449e1"}]}